      "problem": "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+6x-8\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+6x-8.$ We know that $(x+1)$ and $(x-4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $7$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-14$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-7$ | $14$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+6x-8}{x+1}={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+6x-8=(x+1)({x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+6x-8=(x+1)(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}-3x+2=(x-1)(x-2).\n$$",
